<!-- WHAT EACH VALUE LOOKS LIKE IN PRACTICE -->
<section class="cx-scenarios cx-section" id="in-practice">
  <div class="cx-container">
    <div class="r">
      <div class="cx-eyebrow">Values in Practice</div>
      <h2 class="cx-h2">What these words actually<br>mean on a job site.</h2>
      <p class="cx-sub" style="max-width:580px">Anyone can write a value statement. Here is what VNG's values look like when a project hits a wall, which they always do eventually.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cx-scenario-grid stagger">
      <div class="cx-scenario-card r">
        <div class="cx-scenario-label">Accountability: Real Scenario</div>
        <h3>Permit comes back with a comment. Two days lost.</h3>
        <p>Week three of a 7-week office TI. The building permit for the washroom comes back with a comment requiring updated drawings. VNG resequences immediately: frames the open office first, keeps every other trade moving, and picks up the washroom once the revised drawing is approved. The overall timeline does not move.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-scenario-card r">
        <div class="cx-scenario-label">Transparency: Real Scenario</div>
        <h3>Site condition discovered after demo. Scope expands.</h3>
        <p>Demo reveals that the existing slab has a pour-stop that the drawings did not show. It affects the partition layout in one area. We stop, document it, photograph it, and call the PM that day, not at the end of the week. We present two options with cost and schedule impact for each. The client decides. We proceed. No surprises on the invoice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-scenario-card r">
        <div class="cx-scenario-label">Excellence: Real Scenario</div>
        <h3>Drywall finish on a corporate office. Level 4 means Level 4.</h3>
        <p>A project manager specifies Level 4 drywall finish throughout. VNG applies Level 4 across the entire surface, three full coats, sanded to a consistent flatness. Paint under oblique light will reveal every imperfection, and we make sure there are none to reveal. The spec we agreed to is the spec we deliver.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-scenario-card r">
        <div class="cx-scenario-label">Reliability: Real Scenario</div>
        <h3>Millwork delivery date set at week one. Delivered at week seven.</h3>
        <p>The custom reception desk takes five weeks from shop approval to delivery. We place that order on day three of the project, not when we think we need it. We confirm the delivery date with the shop at the start of week six. The millwork arrives Monday morning of week seven, as scheduled. Not because we got lucky. Because we planned for it six weeks earlier.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

